<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morelos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Morelos is a small state of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, with Cuernavaca as its capital. Its warm valleys were settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> peoples and contain the hilltop city of Xochicalco, and in colonial times it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>-growing region. Created in 1869 and named after the independence leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a>, it was the heartland of Emiliano Zapata's revolution from 1911.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morelos.jpg" alt="Morelos" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morelos_in_Mexico_(zoom).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Morelos is landlocked and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> to the north, the State of México to the northeast and northwest, Puebla to the east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> to the southwest. It is the second-smallest state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> after Tlaxcala and has 36 municipalities. The region is a basin into which abundant water flows, and most of it has a warm climate year-round, suited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane and other crops. Visitors from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a> have come to it since Aztec times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first known inhabitants, from about 6000 BCE, were nomadic hunters and gatherers. After the Spanish arrived, agriculture shifted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane and its refining in nearby mills, a system that remained largely intact until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>. Conditions on the sugar plantations made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a>'s call to arms welcome, and rebellions began in 1811. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos y Pavón</a> took over after Hidalgo's execution. In 1812 he and his men held Cuautla for 58 days under royalist siege, an early vital success; he was captured and executed in 1815. In 1869 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-juarez">Benito Juárez</a> separated the territory from the State of México and named it in his honour.</p><p>The state was ready for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>. Emiliano Zapata, born at Anenecuilco, led its best-known movement. His victory in the Battle of Cuautla, 11 to 19 May 1911, brought about the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>. After Francisco Madero's election, Zapata, feeling betrayed, called with his followers for agrarian reform in the Plan de Ayala. The motto Tierra y Libertad was first used by Ricardo Flores Magón in 1910.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cuernavaca, about 90 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, is the capital and is called the City of the Eternal Spring. It has the Palacio de Cortés, which now holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-rivera">Diego Rivera</a>'s mural History of Morelos, Conquest and Revolution. The state is also home to Xochicalco and to the Monasteries on the slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popocatepetl">Popocatépetl</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Morelos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and 500 BCE: in burials at Tlatilco in the Valley of Mexico, on the reliefs of Chalcatzingo in <b>Morelos</b>, in the painted caves of Juxtlahuaca and Oxtotitlán and at Teopantecuanitlan in Guerrero, at San José Mogote in the Valley of Oaxaca, and on carved boulders at Pijijiapan in…</blockquote><a href="/culture/olmecs">Olmecs · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Soldaderas and camp followers, from young girls to middle-aged women and often of mestiza or indigenous origin, came from Oaxaca, <b>Morelos</b>, Tehuantepec, Central Mexico, Sonora and elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-adelita">La Adelita</a></li><li><blockquote>Its fertile lands and chinampas produced maize and other crops, and it controlled routes from the Valley of Mexico to <b>Morelos</b> and Puebla.</blockquote><a href="/state/chalco">Chalco</a></li><li><blockquote>Xochicalco was built around 650 on a group of hills in western <b>Morelos</b>, as power in central Mexico fragmented after the decline of Teotihuacan.</blockquote><a href="/state/xochicalco">Xochicalco</a></li><li><blockquote>Xochimilco was ruled by its own tlatoque and controlled a domain stretching into the hills of <b>Morelos</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/xochimilco">Xochimilco</a></li></ul></section>
